  "headline_findings": [
    "Open-loop governance changed the primary diagnosis in at most 0.0% of cases: the layers annotate but do not alter the decision. Open-loop variants are meant to reuse the baseline's diagnosis, so 0% is expected by design.",
    "Exact McNemar on binary correctness vs V1: 0 of 5 comparisons significant after Holm-Bonferroni over 30 paired tests (smallest raw p 0.039617); accuracy differences range -0.0567 to 0.0167.",
    "Closed vs open loop (V5-CL - V5, n=300): accuracy 0.43 -> 0.3733 (exact McNemar p=0.039617, Holm 0.039617). Within its own runs, revision fixed 21 and broke 39 answers. Both passes started from the same first diagnosis, so the comparison is paired.",
    "47-100% of each governed variant's reported quality drop comes from penalizing its own detectors: the 'Audit Paradox' is a scoring artifact.",
    "No governance signal detects misdiagnosis meaningfully better than chance: AUROCs range 0.497-0.57 (0.5 = chance).",
    "The safety validator raised at least one alert on 100% of runs, so alert presence carries no information about whether the diagnosis is wrong.",
    "Power: with 300 paired cases and 0.1292 mean discordance, the smallest accuracy difference detectable at alpha 0.05 / power 0.8 is 0.0579; a 5-point difference needs 404 pairs (171 baseline misdiagnoses).",
    "JRU is not reported as a measurement: no run was scored by a judge model different from the generator.",
    "Model-reported diagnosis probability: ECE 0.2374, Brier 0.3021 (n=297).",
    "Latency bottleneck: Verifier Agent (Re-check) (mean 22.5s, p95 40.0s)."
  ]
